A notorious beginner bug in Java is placing an accidental semicolon immediately after the `if` condition:

```java
int age = 15;
if (age >= 18); // BUG: Semicolon terminates the if statement immediately as an empty statement!
{
    System.out.println("You can vote!"); // ALWAYS EXECUTES!
}
```

Why this happens:
The compiler treats `if (age >= 18);` as an `if` statement guarding an empty statement (a no-op). The subsequent `{ ... }` block is treated as an independent anonymous block that runs unconditionally on every execution!

