Because evaluation stops at the first `true` condition, inverted condition ordering creates subtle logic bugs:

```java
// --- BROKEN ORDERING ---
if (score >= 60) {
    grade = 'D'; // BUG: A score of 95 satisfies >= 60, getting assigned 'D'!
} else if (score >= 80) {
    grade = 'B'; // UNREACHABLE for any score >= 80!
} else if (score >= 90) {
    grade = 'A'; // UNREACHABLE!
}
```

Rule: Always order numeric range conditions monotonically from most restrictive (highest threshold) to least restrictive (lowest threshold), or vice-versa.

