Deeply nested code (> 3 levels) is known as the 'Pyramid of Doom' or 'Arrow Anti-Pattern'. It pushes code far to the right, making it difficult to read and maintain.

Industry Solution: Guard Clauses (Early Returns / Bouncer Pattern)
Instead of nesting the success cases, check for failures first and return immediately:

```java
// --- CLEAN GUARD CLAUSE PATTERN ---
public String verifyRental(int age, boolean hasLicense, boolean hasInsurance) {
    if (age < 18) return "DENIED: Must be 18+";
    if (!hasLicense) return "DENIED: No license";
    if (!hasInsurance) return "DENIED: No insurance";

    // Main happy path stays flat at indent level 0!
    return "Approved to rent car!";
}
```

