NULL means 'unknown' or 'missing'. It isn't zero and it isn't an empty string. Any comparison with NULL (=, <>, <, >) gives UNKNOWN, not TRUE or FALSE, and WHERE keeps only rows where the condition is TRUE.

To follow along, add a bonus column in the SQL Lab:
ALTER TABLE employees ADD COLUMN bonus INTEGER;
UPDATE employees SET bonus = 5000 WHERE department = 'Engineering';
Now only Alice, Bob and Fiona have a bonus; everyone else has NULL.

